WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:01.960
- Okay, ready guys, audition him.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.628
- It's a lot of pressure here.

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:05.296
(laughter)

00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:06.131
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.131 --> 00:00:07.132
- My name is Nick Barili

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:08.842
and I'm traveling the country to sit down

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.468
with some of the biggest Latinx artists

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:11.845
in places that have meaning to them,

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:13.096
and help tell their stories.

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:15.056
We'll talk about their
journeys and careers,

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:17.100
and explore issues of identity, culture,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.144
discrimination, and representation.

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:20.353
This is "Seen".

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:24.691
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:31.614
♪ Here into the night ooh ♪

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:34.367
- I'm back in LA,

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.577
driving through Bell Gardens,

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:37.620
on my way to Bell Gardens Intermediate

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.122
to meet Mr. Olmos

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:41.166
He actually played a
huge role in the movies

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:44.252
that affected me through my
adolescence and childhood.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:47.255
"Stand and Deliver", "Mi
Familia" and "Selena"

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.716
were all movies that made
me feel proud to be Latino.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.843
Mr. Olmos chose Bell Gardens Intermediate

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.136
as a place for us to meet,

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:56.556
because it's one of the
schools that his program YCP,

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:57.640
Youth Cinema Project,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.810
is actually heavily involved
in integrating film studies

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.854
into public school curriculum.

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:05.148
So excited to talk to
the kids and Mr. Olmos

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.734
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.195
Mr. Olmos

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:11.404
- Hello Nick.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.447
- Thank you for your time.

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:13.364
- You're welcome man.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:14.783
- Do you mind if we take these off?

00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:16.785
- I've got my booster
and all my other shots.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:18.078
- Same, same.

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.872
So of all the places in LA,
this is the one you picked.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:22.457
Why is this place special to you?

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:23.541
- This school district,

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:25.293
the Montebello Unified School District

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:27.754
has been one of the most
prolific school districts

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:28.755
we've had for this program.

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.799
- I wish we had this when I was going up,

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:32.217
because for me when I
moved to this country

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:34.177
not speaking English, I
became really introverted.

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:36.930
I was a kid at the back of
the class who didn't say much,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.181
who was shy to open up my mouth.

00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:42.018
So I think talking and
writing and those things are,

00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:43.394
can be a challenge for someone who English

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:45.063
is a second language, we internalize it.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:46.648
It questions our self-worth,

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:48.566
it plays into so many different layers.

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:50.735
- All of our students end up

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.862
with a much stronger sense of self worth

00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:54.072
when they're done with this program.

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:55.532
We start in the fourth grade.

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:57.659
We tried to at a younger age,

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.828
but they weren't quite ready yet.

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:01.746
First grade, everybody loves school,

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:03.414
second grade, you're
starting to learn stuff,

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:04.749
whether you speak the language you don't,

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:06.417
you're still involved.

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.336
Third grade, becomes the grade

00:02:08.336 --> 00:02:10.463
where they start to read and write,

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.674
and so if you speak
another language first,

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:14.717
and you're not quite up to speed.

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.803
So that wasn't the place

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.221
where we should be at that moment.

00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:20.974
Where we needed to catch
them is in the fourth grade,

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:24.352
when they had now lost
their sense of balance,

00:02:24.352 --> 00:02:27.147
and they were like feeling
less than who they are,

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:30.608
and we bring them into this
program, and they excel.

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:32.026
I can honestly tell you that,

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:35.780
what we've been able to
understand will make you cry.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.532
It brings a sense of emotion

00:02:37.532 --> 00:02:40.034
because you're standing
there looking at kids.

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:41.786
If you saw them just before
they got into the program,

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:43.955
and then you see them during the program,

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:44.789
you would sit there and go,

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:48.418
my God, these kids are so communicative.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:49.878
Of course you're making a movie.

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:52.005
- Right, you have to, you got no choice.

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:54.132
- Yeah, one's a director,
one's a producer,

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.301
one's the writer, one's you
know, the camera person.

00:02:56.301 --> 00:02:59.512
And basically you find
yourself inside of this

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.097
making a movie,

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:03.141
and they all have their stories.

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:05.518
Now we're not teaching them out of a book,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.896
they're teaching us out of their stories.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:09.022
Let's go in the classroom, I'll show you.

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:10.481
- I'd love to.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:13.067
(upbeat music)

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:22.285
- All right, quite on set.

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:24.162
All right, so we're
wrapping up our auditions,

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:25.997
how does everybody feel?

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:26.831
- [Group] Good.

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:27.665
- You're good?

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:28.499
Okay.

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:31.127
So, hi Mr. Olmos how are you?

00:03:31.127 --> 00:03:32.712
- Hey, how are you guys?

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.172
(in Spanish)
How is everybody?

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:35.131
(in Spanish)
Good.

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:36.883
It's good to see you, good to see you.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.760
- We're working on our auditions
for our films this year.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.594
- Really?

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:40.637
- Yeah, if their name is crossed off,

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:41.930
they've already auditioned.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:42.889
- How did they do?

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:43.932
- You guys answer.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:44.766
- [Group] Great.

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:45.767
- Did they do good?

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.060
I'm very proud of you guys.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.811
Are they all in the program?

00:03:48.811 --> 00:03:50.313
- These are our 'YCPeeps'.

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:51.606
- This is my good friend Nick.

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:54.859
He's actually doing an interview on me,

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:57.445
for The Academy of Motion
Pictures, Arts and Sciences.

00:03:57.445 --> 00:03:58.613
And you're?

00:03:58.613 --> 00:03:59.739
- Assistant director for The Heist.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.532
- You are assistant director.

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:02.367
You're?

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:03.201
- I am the producer.

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:04.702
- Producer.

00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:05.995
- I'm the director.

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:07.538
- Congratulations man.

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:08.706
- And you're?

00:04:08.706 --> 00:04:09.791
- I am auditioning.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:10.625
- Right now?

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:11.459
- For the role of Andres.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:12.710
- Well you wanna do it?

00:04:13.878 --> 00:04:14.712
- No pressure.

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:15.838
- It's a lot of pressure here.

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:17.799
(laughter)

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:19.926
- Okay, ready guys, audition him.

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:22.804
- This is a robbery, get down!

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:24.973
Jackson, hurry up, set
up the communication.

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:28.226
Dre, hurry up and set up
the journaling equipment.

00:04:28.226 --> 00:04:29.852
- Can't you see doing just that?

00:04:29.852 --> 00:04:31.396
Besides what are doing just sitting there?

00:04:31.396 --> 00:04:32.772
- Hey don't talk back to me,

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:36.192
I'm you leader, respect me.

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:38.152
- How did you feel about that one?

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:40.863
- I liked it, overall great job.

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:42.365
- Excellent.

00:04:42.365 --> 00:04:44.325
(soft music)

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:45.868
- And you're auditioning right now?

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:46.953
- Yes.

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:47.787
- Let me hear it.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:49.789
- Good morning Danny.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:53.001
- Fatima, you seem really
excited for class today.

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:55.044
- Wait, is there a free period today?

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:56.921
- No silly today Professor
is gonna announce

00:04:56.921 --> 00:04:58.631
the final project of the semester.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.383
- Oh you had to remind me.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:01.676
- It's not that bad.

00:05:01.676 --> 00:05:03.303
I've already organized my full schedule

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:04.345
to fit in with the assignment.

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:06.848
- How can you be so organized?

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:08.474
- I loved it, you did really good.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.643
- I just feel you should
add a bit more movement,

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.729
when you like, oh, you had to remind me.

00:05:12.729 --> 00:05:13.563
- That's good.

00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:15.148
- I think this one is good.

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.232
- Have you directed before?

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.066
- No.

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:18.609
- It's your first time,
how do you like it?

00:05:18.609 --> 00:05:21.654
- I really like it because I
feel like I can make the actors

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.531
like have a better performance.

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:25.366
Like when they come in, they're good,

00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:27.660
but I kind of wants to
give them like adjustments

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.330
on how they could be way
better and on top of it

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.289
and really good.

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.416
(soft music)

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:35.710
- I just wanna thank all of you very much

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.462
for everything you guys are doing.

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:39.380
You guys are truly the future,

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:40.715
and whether you become filmmakers

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:41.841
or you don't become filmmakers,

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:42.967
that's not the issue,

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:45.386
the issue is that you've
gotten the opportunity

00:05:45.386 --> 00:05:49.349
to understand yourselves, your stories,

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.768
and that you learned
self-esteem ,self-respect

00:05:51.768 --> 00:05:52.852
and self-worth.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:56.022
Congratulations all of
you, I'm very proud of you.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.524
- All right, here we
go, a round of applause.

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:00.485
(clapping)

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:01.694
- We'll have to continue
with the conversation,

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:02.528
let's sit down.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:03.321
- Perfect.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:06.616
(soft music)

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:11.746
- Wow, those kids being
able to recite lines

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:13.414
in front of cameras,
that was pretty amazing.

00:06:13.414 --> 00:06:14.248
(laughs)

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:18.211
How does it feel to see the
generation start that young

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:21.214
into the craft that you've
mastered all these decades?

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:22.507
- This is a dream.

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:25.301
You don't quite get it

00:06:25.301 --> 00:06:27.929
until you really
experience the differences,

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:31.557
and these kids have become whole.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:34.977
They're able to look at
you and to talk with you.

00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:37.939
They have confidence,
self-esteem, self-respect,

00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:40.483
and they've only been doing
it for like six months.

00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:43.986
- I always like to start interviews,

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:46.364
kind of like the intro
sequence of a movie.

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:49.575
Like when that pan shot comes
into your childhood home,

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:50.410
what are we seeing?

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:51.244
What are we hearing?

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:52.078
What are we smelling?

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:56.040
- Well, I come from the Ellis
Island of the West Coast,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.166
that's what it's called.

00:06:57.166 --> 00:07:00.294
So my background came from being,

00:07:00.294 --> 00:07:03.923
just a kid in East LA, playing in the,

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:05.466
there was no grass or anything,

00:07:05.466 --> 00:07:07.677
it was just dirt lots and.

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:09.929
It was beautiful, there was a lot of love,

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:14.392
but it was not any kind of
fluent living whatsoever.

00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:16.310
- How did growing up in East LA shape you?

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.354
- Well, it gave me a sense
of balance of who I was

00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:23.109
because we were, you know,
multitude of cultures.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:25.778
So we had indigenous people
living right to my right hand,

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.071
as I come out my front door,

00:07:27.071 --> 00:07:29.407
there was a house with Apache Indians.

00:07:29.407 --> 00:07:32.285
To the left, there was a
family of the Martinez,

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:34.495
they were a Mexican American.

00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:36.664
Right across the street,
we had Japanese family.

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:39.208
They all had kids and all
of us played together.

00:07:39.208 --> 00:07:41.919
And what happened by coming from there,

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:45.965
I was exposed to the clarity of culture.

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:48.384
And I didn't know anything

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:51.596
about any kind of cultural differences,

00:07:51.596 --> 00:07:54.474
discrimination or any kind
of prejudice systems at all.

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:59.228
So we were exposed to religions,
we're exposed to cultures,

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:02.106
and I thought that that's
how the whole world was.

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:04.442
Until I left, I crossed the LA river,

00:08:04.442 --> 00:08:05.443
or the first street bridge,

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:09.155
and I first saw that
there was still Africans,

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.698
but they were in one corner,

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:13.618
Indigenous people were not
even, you couldn't find them.

00:08:13.618 --> 00:08:16.954
And then the Asian community
was stuck in another corner,

00:08:16.954 --> 00:08:20.750
the Latino community was in
Pacoima and in the valley,

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.461
and the Caucasian community
was on the west side,

00:08:23.461 --> 00:08:24.962
and it was quite interesting.

00:08:26.422 --> 00:08:28.049
- Your first love was actually baseball.

00:08:28.049 --> 00:08:30.176
You were a catcher, you
caught Sandy Koufax.

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:31.010
We shared something,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.095
my knees still hurt from
when I used to catch.

00:08:33.095 --> 00:08:35.389
- Eddie Roebuck was the guy
that I really worked with

00:08:35.389 --> 00:08:37.725
the most that during that period
of time, Sandy pitched too.

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.936
But we were playing the
California Sun League,

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:42.563
and I played every day, seven days a week.

00:08:42.563 --> 00:08:45.525
I learned the whole understanding of

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:47.318
you had to spend 10,000 hours.

00:08:47.318 --> 00:08:48.152
- To master.

00:08:48.152 --> 00:08:50.446
- You would just be all that you could be.

00:08:50.446 --> 00:08:53.115
It didn't make you
better than anybody else,

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:55.368
it just made you whole.

00:08:55.368 --> 00:08:58.538
That discipline to do
the things you love to do

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:01.207
when you don't feel like
doing them, came to light.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:02.667
And that was four or five years old

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:04.669
when I stumbled on to this.

00:09:04.669 --> 00:09:08.923
And so by the time I was 12, 11, 12,

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:11.509
I was playing really
strongly and I was catching.

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:14.929
And like you, you know, my knees, oh boy.

00:09:14.929 --> 00:09:17.640
We used to do double headers
and sometimes triple headers.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.101
So I was playing 21 innings a day,

00:09:20.101 --> 00:09:22.311
it was single A ball getting
ready to go into double AA

00:09:22.311 --> 00:09:24.438
and triple AAA and getting
picked up by the majors.

00:09:24.438 --> 00:09:25.481
- That's part of the Dodger system right?

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:26.983
- Yes, that was one of them.

00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:29.318
- So you're on your way

00:09:29.318 --> 00:09:31.696
to become a professional
baseball player, right?

00:09:31.696 --> 00:09:33.573
You're you're in the Dodger system,

00:09:33.573 --> 00:09:36.325
you're 13, you get your
discipline for baseball.

00:09:36.325 --> 00:09:37.243
Then you're like,

00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:39.412
at some point you're like,
I wanna be in a rock band.

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:40.705
What was that moment like,

00:09:40.705 --> 00:09:42.415
and how did you parents feel about that?

00:09:42.415 --> 00:09:44.250
- That moment destroyed my father.

00:09:46.168 --> 00:09:47.545
I'll never forget it.

00:09:47.545 --> 00:09:48.921
He didn't quite get it,

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:51.215
but how could he have gotten it?

00:09:51.215 --> 00:09:52.550
Everybody in the world told him,

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:56.304
you know, this he's a
really natural, natural.

00:09:56.304 --> 00:10:00.266
Natural is just years and
year and years of hard work,

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.226
make you a natural.

00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:04.729
There was nothing natural
about me playing baseball.

00:10:04.729 --> 00:10:05.646
There was nothing natural

00:10:05.646 --> 00:10:07.189
about me being a rock and roll singer,

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:09.900
there was nothing natural
about me being an actor.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:10.943
- What drew to rock and roll?

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:12.737
What was it about rock
and roll that caught you?

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:14.155
- It was the rhythm,

00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:17.825
the intense rhythms and the feeling

00:10:17.825 --> 00:10:19.243
that you got listening to it.

00:10:19.243 --> 00:10:22.705
My father, who was a very good
dancer and my mother was too,

00:10:22.705 --> 00:10:26.751
but he was for me a great,
great asset of understanding

00:10:26.751 --> 00:10:27.585
because he was a,

00:10:27.585 --> 00:10:29.503
he could move.

00:10:29.503 --> 00:10:31.172
And so we would clean the house and stuff,

00:10:31.172 --> 00:10:32.423
he'd put on Perez Prado,

00:10:32.423 --> 00:10:33.716
or he'd put on Tito Puente,

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:38.721
he'd put on all the great
Latin music that there was.

00:10:38.763 --> 00:10:41.474
And while he was cleaning,
we were cleaning and stuff,

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:42.767
he would move.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:44.685
And so we would see him moving

00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:47.271
and we'd start to move with
him, dying of laughter,

00:10:47.271 --> 00:10:48.773
because when you're a little kid

00:10:48.773 --> 00:10:51.400
and you're doing all these
things you never did before,

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:52.943
but you're moving to the music.

00:10:52.943 --> 00:10:55.613
So he got me into music
and that's what happens.

00:10:55.613 --> 00:10:57.198
You get exposed to certain things,

00:10:57.198 --> 00:10:59.241
it changes your perspective on life.

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:02.828
So at 1960, I was playing
baseball every day,

00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:03.704
seven days a week.

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:05.790
But then I realized that, you know what,

00:11:07.917 --> 00:11:10.294
I wanna sing and dance.

00:11:10.294 --> 00:11:11.337
I love baseball,

00:11:11.337 --> 00:11:16.342
but I really love singing
and dancing and moving.

00:11:17.468 --> 00:11:19.845
And so I went into that,

00:11:19.845 --> 00:11:21.013
the same way I played baseball,

00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:23.307
I hung up my cleats, 14 years old,

00:11:23.307 --> 00:11:27.561
I hung up my cleats and my father lost it.

00:11:27.561 --> 00:11:30.523
And it was sad because
he really got hurt by it.

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:31.399
I didn't know how much,

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:33.776
because I was so stuck on my own world.

00:11:33.776 --> 00:11:35.236
I'm gonna go sing and dance,

00:11:35.236 --> 00:11:36.862
you know, what the heck's wrong with you?

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:39.865
But then he realized that
I started doing the music

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:41.325
like I had done baseball,

00:11:41.325 --> 00:11:43.619
and pretty soon I was performing.

00:11:43.619 --> 00:11:46.539
Couldn't sing very well,
but man, could I perform.

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:50.209
I could move and dance using
my Latin and my African roots.

00:11:50.209 --> 00:11:52.169
I would perform seven days a week.

00:11:52.169 --> 00:11:54.088
So we were constantly working.

00:11:54.088 --> 00:11:58.050
And then one day I went
to audition for a play.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:00.720
They asked for two Latino actors to go up

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.973
to the main stages of Los Angeles,

00:12:03.973 --> 00:12:05.015
which has Mark Taper,

00:12:05.015 --> 00:12:07.143
and wow, they sent me as one of the guys.

00:12:07.143 --> 00:12:09.270
And so I went and I didn't get the part,

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.772
but as I was walking
out, I heard a, hey you.

00:12:11.772 --> 00:12:12.815
And I turned around,

00:12:12.815 --> 00:12:14.734
and a miscellaneous girl, young girl,

00:12:14.734 --> 00:12:16.819
and she goes, "Yeah you, you yeah, yeah.

00:12:16.819 --> 00:12:17.653
I'm talking to you.

00:12:17.653 --> 00:12:20.281
Do you want to try out for
a play that we're doing?"

00:12:20.281 --> 00:12:22.324
I said, "What play?"

00:12:22.324 --> 00:12:24.076
She goes, "Do you or don't you?"

00:12:24.076 --> 00:12:26.537
I go, "Yes I do, yes I do."

00:12:26.537 --> 00:12:28.497
I could see that she was like questioning

00:12:28.497 --> 00:12:30.291
why she even stopped me.

00:12:30.291 --> 00:12:32.835
And so she goes, I go,
"Yes I would thank you."

00:12:32.835 --> 00:12:36.881
She goes, "Okay be here on
a Monday at three o'clock."

00:12:36.881 --> 00:12:39.216
So I left, came back on
Monday at three o'clock,

00:12:39.216 --> 00:12:41.761
it was hundreds of guys there.

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:44.221
And there was this play called "Zoot Suit"

00:12:44.221 --> 00:12:45.765
so I got to do that.

00:12:45.765 --> 00:12:50.186
That was a combination of
using theater, movement,

00:12:50.186 --> 00:12:53.898
singing, all of the things
that I had been doing

00:12:53.898 --> 00:12:56.150
day in and day out for years.

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:58.694
'Cause I've been doing theater since 1964,

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:00.279
I got in my first acting class

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.239
in East LA Community College.

00:13:02.239 --> 00:13:03.741
- Can you speak a little bit about

00:13:03.741 --> 00:13:05.701
what went into that character.

00:13:05.701 --> 00:13:06.786
What were all the elements

00:13:06.786 --> 00:13:09.121
that came into you crafting Pachuco?

00:13:09.121 --> 00:13:11.332
- Everything that I have
been working towards

00:13:11.332 --> 00:13:14.418
and understanding through my
life, were funneled into this,

00:13:14.418 --> 00:13:16.754
I could use it in this performance.

00:13:16.754 --> 00:13:20.174
This character became
capable of using it all,

00:13:20.174 --> 00:13:22.176
I mean, it was amazing what happened

00:13:22.176 --> 00:13:24.512
with that character. El Pachuco,

00:13:24.512 --> 00:13:27.306
to this day, is still profoundly

00:13:27.306 --> 00:13:29.225
one of the most beloved characters

00:13:29.225 --> 00:13:31.060
to rise on the American stage

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:32.561
in the history of American theater.

00:13:32.561 --> 00:13:33.938
Not me saying that,

00:13:33.938 --> 00:13:35.898
that was the American Theater who said it.

00:13:37.066 --> 00:13:38.901
And I mean, it was the character,

00:13:38.901 --> 00:13:41.904
granted I was capable of
handling the responsibility,

00:13:41.904 --> 00:13:44.240
which was, thank the Lord I was ready

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:45.825
because we really needed it.

00:13:45.825 --> 00:13:48.869
The year was 1978 and
there had never been a play

00:13:48.869 --> 00:13:50.871
dealing with the Latino culture

00:13:50.871 --> 00:13:52.206
other than "West Side Story".

00:13:52.206 --> 00:13:55.835
But studying the Puerto
Rican in "West Side Story",

00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:59.588
was like studying the
Polynesian in "South Pacific".

00:13:59.588 --> 00:14:02.007
It wasn't meant to study the culture,

00:14:02.007 --> 00:14:04.677
it was meant to use the
culture to tell a story,

00:14:04.677 --> 00:14:05.761
which was fine.

00:14:05.761 --> 00:14:09.265
This was the first time the
culture had ever spoken.

00:14:09.265 --> 00:14:11.642
This was a culture that spoke,

00:14:11.642 --> 00:14:14.603
and what it said was monumental.

00:14:14.603 --> 00:14:16.856
That play changed the perspectives

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:19.900
and the understandings
of the Latino culture

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.861
in this country and opened it up.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:24.363
- How did it feel for a
group of kids in East LA,

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:25.990
not only do you put on for the city,

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.409
but then go to Broadway and send ripples

00:14:28.409 --> 00:14:29.243
throughout the world.

00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:31.203
I mean, I read that people were coming in

00:14:31.203 --> 00:14:33.163
from like London Theater
and Moscow Theater,

00:14:33.163 --> 00:14:34.248
and all these different places,

00:14:34.248 --> 00:14:35.958
just to see what you guys were doing.

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:36.959
- Yeah, they did.

00:14:36.959 --> 00:14:39.336
And they came when we were here in LA.

00:14:39.336 --> 00:14:42.131
And what the shot, was
heard around the world,

00:14:42.131 --> 00:14:45.551
that's how strong the piece was.

00:14:45.551 --> 00:14:48.262
I have to give it to Louis
and to Gordon Davidson,

00:14:48.262 --> 00:14:51.223
those two giants of the American theater,

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:53.642
where the people who really created this,

00:14:53.642 --> 00:14:55.394
and allowed it to be born.

00:14:55.394 --> 00:14:57.980
- I read that you did 13 years of theater

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:00.107
without getting a paycheck, is that true?

00:15:00.107 --> 00:15:00.941
- Yeah, yeah.

00:15:00.941 --> 00:15:01.859
- All right, can you speak on that,

00:15:01.859 --> 00:15:03.652
'cause I feel like people
hear that and they're like,

00:15:03.652 --> 00:15:05.863
you have to really love something

00:15:05.863 --> 00:15:07.573
to do for 13 years for nothing.

00:15:07.573 --> 00:15:08.407
- For nothing.

00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:09.867
I played baseball for nothing.

00:15:09.867 --> 00:15:12.077
- I mean you're in this rock band,

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:12.912
you're not getting paid,

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:14.246
you're doing theater,
you're not getting paid.

00:15:14.246 --> 00:15:16.999
Like you probably have other
jobs that are paying the bills,

00:15:16.999 --> 00:15:17.833
while you're doing all this.

00:15:17.833 --> 00:15:19.084
- I used to deliver furniture.

00:15:19.084 --> 00:15:19.919
(laughs)

00:15:19.919 --> 00:15:22.004
- You delivered furniture?

00:15:22.004 --> 00:15:24.506
- Yeah, yeah, I mean that's
how I supported myself

00:15:24.506 --> 00:15:25.633
for a long time too.

00:15:26.675 --> 00:15:29.762
I had a van from playing
in rock and roll music,

00:15:29.762 --> 00:15:33.098
you know, between the
situations of working on stage,

00:15:33.098 --> 00:15:35.726
I would deliver like two
o'clock in the morning,

00:15:35.726 --> 00:15:36.560
three o'clock in the morning,

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.104
I'd be delivering furniture from auctions.

00:15:39.104 --> 00:15:39.939
- Wow.

00:15:39.939 --> 00:15:42.733
So you're performing
and then a two o'clock

00:15:42.733 --> 00:15:44.068
you're going to deliver furniture.

00:15:44.068 --> 00:15:46.070
- Yeah, from two to seven.

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:47.321
- That's dedication.

00:15:47.321 --> 00:15:49.073
I feel like one of the
most empowering things

00:15:49.073 --> 00:15:50.199
of your career,

00:15:50.199 --> 00:15:51.742
is that you found a way to make

00:15:51.742 --> 00:15:53.535
all the characters you played your own.

00:15:53.535 --> 00:15:55.162
How are you able to accomplish that?

00:15:55.162 --> 00:15:57.665
- Since El Pachuco, I've
had creative control

00:15:57.665 --> 00:15:58.666
of my characters.

00:15:58.666 --> 00:16:00.542
And no one argues, no one says,

00:16:00.542 --> 00:16:03.212
you know, Eddie, no,
no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:16:03.212 --> 00:16:05.923
I could have never worked
with Alfred Hitchcock,

00:16:05.923 --> 00:16:08.634
he didn't need anybody
else to add to his movies.

00:16:08.634 --> 00:16:11.929
Okay, I get it, that's perfect, go for it.

00:16:11.929 --> 00:16:12.763
Just don't call me,

00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:14.682
because I might think of something

00:16:14.682 --> 00:16:18.268
that might enhance your story.

00:16:18.268 --> 00:16:19.770
And that's all I do, I mean,

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.439
there's some times when
I never changed a dot,

00:16:22.439 --> 00:16:24.483
a period, nothing.

00:16:24.483 --> 00:16:29.113
I do exactly what's on the page
because it's perfectly done.

00:16:29.113 --> 00:16:30.823
And then sometimes I'll augment it

00:16:30.823 --> 00:16:34.118
and move it to another level
and they're very grateful.

00:16:35.077 --> 00:16:37.204
- Take me to the "Stand and Deliver".

00:16:37.204 --> 00:16:38.956
Tell me a little bit about
how that came to be made,

00:16:38.956 --> 00:16:42.292
'cause I heard that it took
five years for that to happen.

00:16:42.292 --> 00:16:43.127
- Yeah.

00:16:43.127 --> 00:16:44.211
- You guys filmed it
independently, is that correct?

00:16:44.211 --> 00:16:45.337
- Yes, yes we did.

00:16:45.337 --> 00:16:47.131
- Anyone who sees it, it's
such a powerful story,

00:16:47.131 --> 00:16:48.799
but when you pitched it to Hollywood,

00:16:48.799 --> 00:16:50.759
what were they telling you in terms of why

00:16:50.759 --> 00:16:51.885
they couldn't finance it?

00:16:51.885 --> 00:16:56.181
- Well, basically the one
line that really just like,

00:16:56.181 --> 00:16:57.266
hit us like a ton of bricks.

00:16:57.266 --> 00:17:00.394
So we would go like the NBC or CBS,

00:17:00.394 --> 00:17:01.228
then suddenly NBC said,

00:17:01.228 --> 00:17:03.647
you know, the day that
you wanna do this story,

00:17:03.647 --> 00:17:05.524
which is a wonderful
story, don't get me wrong.

00:17:05.524 --> 00:17:06.775
And they say, well, you know,

00:17:06.775 --> 00:17:09.028
they'll play "Terminator"
on the same night

00:17:09.028 --> 00:17:10.779
that were playing "Stand and Deliver",

00:17:10.779 --> 00:17:12.698
what do you think people are gonna watch?

00:17:12.698 --> 00:17:17.119
And I said, "Stand and
Deliver" it's a better story.

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:20.873
And then they chuckled and
they let us leave the room.

00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:22.249
No we got our money funding

00:17:22.249 --> 00:17:24.793
from the Public Broadcasting, PBS,

00:17:24.793 --> 00:17:26.879
and the American Playhouse,

00:17:26.879 --> 00:17:28.422
which started the seed money.

00:17:28.422 --> 00:17:31.675
It costs us about a million
two to do the movie.

00:17:31.675 --> 00:17:32.509
- Wow.

00:17:32.509 --> 00:17:33.802
- So we got 10,000 from this person,

00:17:33.802 --> 00:17:35.262
15,000 from this person,

00:17:35.262 --> 00:17:37.681
200,000 from this person.

00:17:37.681 --> 00:17:41.226
You know, different people
would give us to make up

00:17:41.226 --> 00:17:44.354
for the 1.2 million that we
needed to make the movie.

00:17:44.354 --> 00:17:47.149
We had captured lightning in a bottle.

00:17:48.275 --> 00:17:51.612
And I'm very grateful
because that movie has helped

00:17:51.612 --> 00:17:53.530
so many people.

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:55.449
- I saw it recently and
it definitely stands

00:17:55.449 --> 00:17:56.283
the test of time.

00:17:56.283 --> 00:17:57.493
What do you think it is about the movie

00:17:57.493 --> 00:17:59.828
that can resonate from
generation to generation?

00:17:59.828 --> 00:18:01.663
- I think it's the story.

00:18:01.663 --> 00:18:02.539
The story itself,

00:18:02.539 --> 00:18:06.460
the truth of the story itself is powerful.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.420
A hundred years from today,

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:12.382
that movie will be seen and
understood and be appreciated.

00:18:12.382 --> 00:18:14.551
All we wanted to do is just document

00:18:14.551 --> 00:18:16.512
the behavior of this man.

00:18:16.512 --> 00:18:17.471
And we did it,

00:18:17.471 --> 00:18:19.723
and I was very fortunate
I was able to do it.

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:24.103
- [Narrator] Edward James Olmos
for a "Stand and Deliver".

00:18:24.103 --> 00:18:26.063
- We did not have Spanish surnames

00:18:26.063 --> 00:18:29.024
and come from Barrio school you know that.

00:18:29.024 --> 00:18:30.234
There's something going on here

00:18:30.234 --> 00:18:32.111
that nobody's talking about,

00:18:32.111 --> 00:18:33.862
and you know what it is.

00:18:33.862 --> 00:18:36.406
I know well how to spell discrimination.

00:18:36.406 --> 00:18:38.826
I thought this was over a long time ago.

00:18:38.826 --> 00:18:40.869
Are you doing this to my kids?

00:18:40.869 --> 00:18:44.414
My kids could teach you
a thing or two Johnny.

00:18:44.414 --> 00:18:45.457
- I'm gonna call security

00:18:45.457 --> 00:18:47.459
if you can't control yourself Escalante.

00:18:47.459 --> 00:18:48.293
- Go for it.

00:18:49.336 --> 00:18:51.380
(clapping)

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:56.051
- That movie received a nomination
for a best leading actor.

00:18:56.051 --> 00:19:00.222
You are the only US born
Latino to be nominated

00:19:00.222 --> 00:19:02.850
for best actor or best actress.

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:03.976
- In the history of the Academy.

00:19:03.976 --> 00:19:05.435
- In the history of the Academy.

00:19:05.435 --> 00:19:09.022
Why do you think it's been such a struggle

00:19:09.022 --> 00:19:12.901
to get another US born
Latino to that stage

00:19:12.901 --> 00:19:14.069
where they could be nominated.

00:19:14.069 --> 00:19:16.488
Latino or Latina, because
that applies to both.

00:19:16.488 --> 00:19:18.866
- Either, it's just a story.

00:19:18.866 --> 00:19:21.869
The roles are what define it.

00:19:21.869 --> 00:19:26.874
And it's been very hard
to get that opportunity.

00:19:27.457 --> 00:19:29.668
Like for instance,

00:19:29.668 --> 00:19:31.545
"Argo" a beautiful movie,

00:19:31.545 --> 00:19:34.381
best movie of the year when it came out.

00:19:34.381 --> 00:19:37.676
Got all kinds of accolades for
Ben Affleck and everything,

00:19:37.676 --> 00:19:40.971
but it didn't win him the Academy Award.

00:19:40.971 --> 00:19:43.182
Had he used a Latino.

00:19:43.182 --> 00:19:45.392
- Which is what the story was about.

00:19:45.392 --> 00:19:47.186
- Yeah, it was about a Mexican American,

00:19:47.186 --> 00:19:48.812
but nobody ever knew it.

00:19:48.812 --> 00:19:51.148
Tony Mendez was the character
that they portrayed,

00:19:51.148 --> 00:19:52.691
but they used his name a couple of times,

00:19:52.691 --> 00:19:55.194
but it's so thrown away that most people,

00:19:55.194 --> 00:19:56.403
you could ask them,

00:19:56.403 --> 00:19:59.615
did you know that this was
a Mexican American person?

00:19:59.615 --> 00:20:01.116
It was the Mexican American culture.

00:20:01.116 --> 00:20:02.576
No, no.

00:20:02.576 --> 00:20:04.870
- You've been a strong
advocate for representation

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:08.040
for a long time, why is
that important for you?

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.043
- To me, trying to understand your life

00:20:11.043 --> 00:20:14.796
and then your culture, and your heritage,

00:20:14.796 --> 00:20:17.966
and the roots that make
you the tree that you are,

00:20:17.966 --> 00:20:20.469
and the human being that you are,

00:20:20.469 --> 00:20:24.014
sharing it so that
others can understand it,

00:20:24.014 --> 00:20:26.016
makes everybody stronger.

00:20:26.016 --> 00:20:28.560
And that's why I'm a storyteller,

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:29.895
and that's why I got into this,

00:20:29.895 --> 00:20:32.731
and that's why I stand
here today telling stories,

00:20:32.731 --> 00:20:36.777
and trying desperately hard to listen.

00:20:36.777 --> 00:20:38.654
Listening is the key.

00:20:38.654 --> 00:20:40.447
I listened to you Nick,

00:20:40.447 --> 00:20:42.991
you're very, very educated
in respects of knowing

00:20:42.991 --> 00:20:44.284
who and what I am.

00:20:44.284 --> 00:20:47.913
And I'm very proud of the fact
that I'm being interviewed

00:20:47.913 --> 00:20:52.000
by someone who really
understands who I am,

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.919
and it makes a big difference.

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:56.672
And I appreciate you doing
the work that you've done

00:20:56.672 --> 00:20:59.466
to understand what
you're doing here today.

00:20:59.466 --> 00:21:03.887
- You don't know how seen
I feel at this moment.

00:21:03.887 --> 00:21:06.306
I feel like it's been 25
years to get on the seat,

00:21:06.306 --> 00:21:07.224
just like you said,

00:21:07.224 --> 00:21:09.476
the 13 years of theater that
no one paid you or whatever,

00:21:09.476 --> 00:21:12.854
it's like it all comes
to this place right now.

00:21:12.854 --> 00:21:14.022
- Yes it does.

00:21:14.022 --> 00:21:15.357
- Throughout your career,

00:21:15.357 --> 00:21:16.608
you've not only acted,

00:21:16.608 --> 00:21:19.569
you've directed, you've
written stuff, you've produced,

00:21:19.569 --> 00:21:21.863
why is it important for
you to wear multiple hats?

00:21:21.863 --> 00:21:25.993
- Out of a necessity to
stay alive in this industry,

00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:30.247
I had to learn the full
usage of storytelling.

00:21:30.247 --> 00:21:34.376
Whether it be in songwriting,
filmmaking, or theater.

00:21:34.376 --> 00:21:35.877
If we weren't creating it,

00:21:35.877 --> 00:21:37.254
we weren't gonna be able to do it,

00:21:37.254 --> 00:21:38.547
because nobody was creating it for us.

00:21:38.547 --> 00:21:40.299
- Seen what you've seen,

00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:42.384
and experienced what you've experienced,

00:21:42.384 --> 00:21:46.471
what will you tell a young
actor, director, writer,

00:21:46.471 --> 00:21:49.099
Latino, Latina who's watching this,

00:21:49.099 --> 00:21:51.727
who maybe hasn't seen as much
of themselves represented

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:54.813
when they turn on the
streamers or go to the movies,

00:21:54.813 --> 00:21:56.356
but who wants to do it,

00:21:56.356 --> 00:21:58.066
what advice would you have for them?

00:21:58.066 --> 00:22:00.527
- The strongest advice
I can give any artists

00:22:00.527 --> 00:22:05.532
in any perspective,
disciplines, is don't stop.

00:22:06.074 --> 00:22:09.161
Don't let anything stop you, die doing it.

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.539
You know, just don't stop.

00:22:12.539 --> 00:22:14.666
You know, success is not money,

00:22:14.666 --> 00:22:17.085
it's not fame, it's not fortune,

00:22:17.085 --> 00:22:19.546
success is having lived your life

00:22:19.546 --> 00:22:22.007
doing what you really want it to do,

00:22:22.007 --> 00:22:23.717
and doing it to the best of your ability.

00:22:23.717 --> 00:22:27.095
That means you spend thousands of hours

00:22:27.095 --> 00:22:29.139
doing it in this lifetime.

00:22:29.139 --> 00:22:31.016
Just don't stop,

00:22:31.016 --> 00:22:33.310
don't let anything stop you.

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.270
The key is to do the things you wanna do

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:37.147
when you don't feel like doing them.

00:22:37.147 --> 00:22:39.858
So the moment you wanna stop
and say, this is too much,

00:22:39.858 --> 00:22:41.943
I can't take the rejection or,

00:22:41.943 --> 00:22:44.071
you know, no one really
cares about my music,

00:22:44.071 --> 00:22:45.364
nobody cares about my movies,

00:22:45.364 --> 00:22:46.698
nobody cares about me,

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:47.991
nobody cares about anything.

00:22:47.991 --> 00:22:51.203
Ah, I don't care about me,

00:22:51.203 --> 00:22:53.288
I don't care about myself, I'm stopping,

00:22:54.247 --> 00:22:57.209
that never can you do.

00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:01.046
You must never stop.

00:23:01.046 --> 00:23:02.798
- You've had iconic roles,

00:23:02.798 --> 00:23:05.926
you help found LALIFF, Youth Cinema,

00:23:05.926 --> 00:23:08.095
Latino Public Broadcast.

00:23:08.095 --> 00:23:10.305
We can go on and on and on and on,

00:23:10.305 --> 00:23:12.724
what's left to accomplish for you?

00:23:12.724 --> 00:23:15.018
- When I pass and leave this world,

00:23:15.018 --> 00:23:18.605
I could have done way more work,

00:23:18.605 --> 00:23:20.232
'cause I would've been
offered a lot of work,

00:23:20.232 --> 00:23:22.692
more work I've been
offered than I've taken.

00:23:22.692 --> 00:23:23.860
People say, why did he do that?

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:28.532
You could have been a lot
more famous and a lot richer.

00:23:28.532 --> 00:23:29.408
(laughs)

00:23:29.408 --> 00:23:31.451
I said, yeah, but in essence,

00:23:31.451 --> 00:23:34.246
I wouldn't have felt the way
I feel right this moment.

00:23:34.246 --> 00:23:35.872
I have movies that I'd like to make,

00:23:35.872 --> 00:23:38.333
that I've been trying
to make for 35 years,

00:23:38.333 --> 00:23:40.293
that I still haven't been able to make.

00:23:41.253 --> 00:23:42.838
And I hope that I can make them,

00:23:42.838 --> 00:23:45.006
and if I don't, I'm gonna die trying,

00:23:45.006 --> 00:23:46.842
and all I can tell you.

00:23:46.842 --> 00:23:48.176
- Thank you for your time.

00:23:48.176 --> 00:23:50.595
Thank you for all the
doors that you've opened

00:23:50.595 --> 00:23:53.348
and knocked down for generations to come.

00:23:53.348 --> 00:23:54.975
Thank you for full circle moment,

00:23:54.975 --> 00:23:56.643
we're we're here in the classroom,

00:23:56.643 --> 00:23:58.395
"Stand and Deliver" was so impactful,

00:23:58.395 --> 00:24:00.772
and now we're actually
in a classroom with kids

00:24:00.772 --> 00:24:02.691
and impacting a whole new generation.

00:24:02.691 --> 00:24:04.651
Thank you for all that you do.

00:24:04.651 --> 00:24:05.944
My honor, my privilege,

00:24:05.944 --> 00:24:08.113
thank you for your
kindness and your respect.

00:24:08.113 --> 00:24:11.116
(soft music)

00:24:11.116 --> 00:24:13.535
- It's hard to even put what
just happened in the words.

00:24:13.535 --> 00:24:18.081
I feel all this like rush
of energy coming through me.

00:24:18.081 --> 00:24:20.584
I just feel gratitude from Mr. Olmos

00:24:20.584 --> 00:24:21.877
for sharing his time,

00:24:21.877 --> 00:24:25.338
and the moment where he just
acknowledged the research

00:24:25.338 --> 00:24:26.548
and just the work.

00:24:26.548 --> 00:24:28.592
You know, being in this
industry for 25 years,

00:24:28.592 --> 00:24:30.427
you don't really have a
lot of moments like that,

00:24:30.427 --> 00:24:31.678
where you actually feel like

00:24:31.678 --> 00:24:34.890
what I'm doing is being
seen by this person

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:36.808
that I look up to and have
admired my whole life.

00:24:36.808 --> 00:24:39.728
So can't even know what the
next episode is gonna be

00:24:39.728 --> 00:24:41.021
and how we're gonna be
able to top this one.

00:24:41.021 --> 00:24:44.316
We're gonna find a way to
bring this level of energy

00:24:44.316 --> 00:24:47.152
to every episode that we do.

00:24:47.152 --> 00:24:49.738
(upbeat music)

